      <h1>See where your disk has gone.</h1>
      <p class="scope-lede">Scope scans a full SSD in under a minute and draws every folder as a map you can read: the worktrees, environments, model stores and caches that coding agents and local models leave behind, and everything else beside them. Find what takes the space, see what getting it back would cost, then send it to the Trash.</p>
      <div class="scope-cta">
        <a class="button" href="{{ appStore }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download on the Mac App Store</a>
        <p>macOS 14 or later · One purchase, no subscription</p>
      </div>
      <img class="scope-shot" src="/assets/scope/1-map.png" alt="Scope's window showing a 1 TB volume as a treemap, with Users, Applications and Library as the largest rectangles, and a summary reading 100.5 GB you could clear, 57.1 GB of it in under a minute" width="1440" height="900">
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap scope-reasons">
      <p><strong>Fast.</strong> The map appears within a second and fills in as the scan runs. Nine million entries scan in 35 seconds on an M4 Pro.</p>
      <p><strong>Honest.</strong> A folder Scope cannot read is drawn and counted, not skipped. Sizes are what files take on disk, and every size says what clearing it frees. The total says what it could and could not see, and "Elsewhere on this disk" breaks down the rest.</p>
      <p><strong>Yours.</strong> No account, no network, no telemetry. Your scan never leaves your Mac. Scope moves nothing without asking, and never runs a command.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Two ways to look</h2>
      <div class="scope-pair">
        <div>
          <img class="scope-shot" src="/assets/scope/3-types.png" alt="The Developer folder as a flat treemap coloured by file type" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy">
          <p><strong>The flat map.</strong> A treemap in the classic SpaceMonger style: every folder a labelled rectangle sized by what it holds, its subfolders nested inside. Click to zoom in; the breadcrumb takes you back.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <img class="scope-shot" src="/assets/scope/4-3d-types.png" alt="The same Developer folder as a 3D platter of blocks, coloured by file type" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy">
          <p><strong>The 3D map.</strong> The same disk as a platter of blocks. Orbit, zoom and pan. Each block's height is its size, or its age. Switch with one click and keep your place, your selection and your filters.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Colour it the way you need</h2>
      <ul class="scope-modes">
        <li class="mode-depth"><strong>Depth</strong> shows how deep each folder sits.</li>
        <li class="mode-type"><strong>Type</strong> tells code, build output and caches, media, archives and images, apps, and models and data apart.</li>
        <li class="mode-age"><strong>Age</strong> shows what you have not touched in months.</li>
        <li class="mode-clearable"><strong>Clearable</strong> picks out build output, package caches, virtual environments and agent worktrees: space you can get back and rebuild. Each entry says its way back, from "rebuilds itself" to a download timed at your connection's speed.</li>
        <li class="mode-change"><strong>Change</strong> compares with a saved scan: what grew, what shrank, what is new and what is gone. Play steps through every saved scan as a time-lapse.</li>
        <li class="mode-growth"><strong>Growth</strong> shows what is growing now, while Scope watches the volume.</li>
        <li class="mode-origin"><strong>Origin</strong> groups the disk by the tool that wrote each folder: a coding agent, a model runner, a container engine, Xcode, a package manager, or you.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="scope-note">Filter by name, kind or age to see only the caches, the videos or the files older than a year. The sidebar lists every folder by size as an outline, and the Developer view lists your projects.</p>
      <div class="scope-pair">
        <img class="scope-shot" src="/assets/scope/5-ages.png" alt="The volume coloured by age, from recent to years old" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy">
        <img class="scope-shot" src="/assets/scope/8-filter.png" alt="The folder filter matching Developer, with the rest of the map dimmed" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy">
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Free the space, carefully</h2>
      <div class="scope-pair">
        <ul class="scope-list">
          <li><strong>Move to Trash</strong>, one entry or a selection of several, with Undo. The sheet names the path, the size and what the space comes back as, and warns before data macOS or your apps keep, or a worktree with changes not yet committed.</li>
          <li><strong>Empty What Scope Moved</strong> removes only what Scope moved there, after asking. Scope never deletes permanently without asking, and never what it did not move.</li>
          <li><strong>The tool's own command.</strong> Where the Trash is the wrong tool, such as an agent worktree or Docker's disk image, Scope shows the owning tool's clean-up command to copy. It never runs one.</li>
          <li><strong>Rescan Folder</strong> scans one folder again and updates every size above it. No full rescan after a clear-out.</li>
          <li><strong>Reveal in Finder</strong>, open with the app you choose, or copy the path.</li>
        </ul>
        <img class="scope-shot" src="/assets/scope/6-trash.png" alt="The Move to Trash sheet for DerivedData, 38.4 GB, saying the space comes back when the Trash is emptied" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy">
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Keep it from filling again</h2>
      <ul class="scope-cards">
        <li><strong>Watch</strong> a volume and a menu bar item reads the free space and says when the disk will be full. Its menu lists the five fastest-growing folders with their rate. A notification warns when the forecast falls under your threshold, or when one file grows fast; choosing it opens Scope at the cause.</li>
        <li><strong>Standing orders</strong> are rules you write once: agent worktrees not changed for 14 days with nothing left to commit, build output of projects not changed for 60 days, package folders of projects not changed for 90 days. A rule moves nothing on its own. The weekly review lists what the rules match, you approve, and one Undo puts it all back.</li>
        <li><strong>Low space.</strong> When free space falls under the figure you set, Scope lists the quickest space to get back from your last scan, cheapest way back first.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="scope-pair scope-pair-text">
        <div>
          <h2>Put a project to sleep</h2>
          <p>The Developer view lists the projects on the disk with what could be cleared and when you last worked on them. <strong>Hibernate</strong> moves what can be rebuilt to the Trash, keeps your own files and anything not yet committed, and leaves a receipt in the project that names the build command. Undo wakes it.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2>Hand it over</h2>
          <ul class="scope-plain">
            <li><strong>Export Summary</strong> writes the 300 largest folders and the totals as text to paste, or every folder as JSON, with your folder names hidden if you choose. Nothing is sent anywhere.</li>
            <li><strong>Elsewhere on this disk</strong> explains the space the disk says is used that the scan did not find: other volumes, local snapshots by date, the folders Scope could not read, and what is left, named as such.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Made for people whose AI tools fill the disk</h2>
      <ul class="scope-list">
        <li><strong>Colour by Clearable</strong> picks out what a coding agent leaves: its worktrees and the virtual environments, build output and package caches around them. Each says what getting it back would cost.</li>
        <li><strong>Colour by Origin</strong> says which tool wrote each folder, so you see what the agent, the model runner and the container engine each take.</li>
        <li><strong>Colour by Type</strong> shows model weights, datasets and agent transcripts as Models and data, so a local model store is seen for what it is.</li>
        <li><strong>Colour by Change</strong> shows what grew since your last scan, so you see what an agent wrote overnight.</li>
        <li><strong>Every size says what clearing it frees</strong>: a store that hard-links or clones its files into ten projects counts once.</li>
        <li><strong>Empty What Scope Moved</strong> gives the space back without leaving Scope, and touches nothing else in the Trash.</li>
      </ul>
      <img class="scope-shot scope-shot-after" src="/assets/scope/2-3d.png" alt="The whole volume as a 3D platter: Users as the tallest block beside Applications and Library, with the free space as a flat slab" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy">
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap scope-text">
      <h2>Made for developers, good for everyone</h2>
      <p><code>node_modules</code>, <code>DerivedData</code>, <code>.venv</code>, agent worktrees and package caches are what quietly fill a developer's disk, and Scope shows them first. The same map finds the forgotten video exports and the photo library you copied twice.</p>
      <p>Using a coding agent? <a href="https://ayeo.io/scope/connector">Scope for agents</a> lets it ask before it fills the disk, and lets you decide what goes.</p>

      <h2>What Scope is not</h2>
      <p class="scope-not">Not an optimiser. Scope names what you could clear and what getting it back would cost, then does what you pick. It never picks for you, and it never runs a command.</p>

